</figure>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical Validation of Results</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted statistical tests comparing the performance of our method to that of the linear classifier across 12 training–evaluation dataset combinations of generalization. Specifically, we followed the guidelines of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dror et al. (2018)</a></cite> and applied a permutation test (10,000 iterations) and McNemar’s test to evaluate whether our method achieves significantly better accuracy (<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With threshold calibration, the direction significantly outperformed the classifier in 91.67% (11/12) of combinations on Llama-3-8B-Instruct and 83.33% (10/12) on Gemma-3-12B-IT, on both the permutation and McNemar’s tests (Table <a href="#A3.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Appendix C Combining Directions Across Layers and Token Positions ‣ Detecting (Un)answerability in Large Language Modelswith Linear Directions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Without threshold calibration, the direction outperformed the classifier in 58.33% (7/12) of combinations for both models under the permutation test, and in 50% (6/12) for Llama-3-8B-Instruct and 58.33% (7/12) for Gemma-3-12B-IT under McNemar’s test (Table <a href="#A3.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ Appendix C Combining Directions Across Layers and Token Positions ‣ Detecting (Un)answerability in Large Language Modelswith Linear Directions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). Overall, these results show that our method finds directions that often outperform the classifier, with consistent significant improvements after calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Combining Directions Across Layers and Token Positions</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted an experiment testing whether combining two directions from different layers and token positions improves performance. Specifically, we selected the top two vectors (based on maximum steering scores), taken from different layers and token positions, and for classification calculated the two corresponding dot products and summed the scores. Table <a href="#A3.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Appendix C Combining Directions Across Layers and Token Positions ‣ Detecting (Un)answerability in Large Language Modelswith Linear Directions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> shows the averaged F1 scores across all dataset combinations for both our original method and the two-vector method, as well as the average difference. Overall, we see that combining two directions doesn’t lead to substantial gains in performance.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
